<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dumbarton Rock</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Alt Clut</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dumbarton Rock is a volcanic plug on the north bank of the Clyde in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, known in Brittonic as Alt Clut, 'rock of the Clyde'. It was the chief fortress of the British kings of Strathclyde from at least the 5th century until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> besieged and sacked it in 870, after which the kingdom's centre moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/govan">Govan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dumbarton-rock.jpg" alt="Dumbarton Rock" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scotland_Dumbarton_Castle_bordercropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early Middle Ages the Picts fought the Gaels of Dál Riata, the Britons of Alt Clut (<b>Dumbarton</b>) and the Angles of Northumbria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>They are generally regarded as ancestors of the British people of the Clyde, whose Kingdom of Strathclyde was centred on Alt Clut, <b>Dumbarton Rock</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/damnonii">Damnonii</a></li><li><blockquote>Its early king Ceretic, probably the Coroticus whom St Patrick rebuked for slave-raiding in the 5th century, ruled from Alt Clut, the fortress on <b>Dumbarton Rock</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-strathclyde">Kingdom of Strathclyde</a></li></ul></section>
